root=Column(/"EPF vs PPF vs NPS","h1",/"m3d")
=Table(columns=[{Feature:"Feature","EPF","PPF","NPS"},{Feature:"Eligibility","Salaried employees in EP-covered establishments; mandatory for many-sector workers.","Any resident individual can open an account.","Indian citizens aged 18–70; both salaried and self-employed can join"},{Feature:"Contribution rules","Employee contributes 2% of basic + dearness allowance; employer also contributes.","₹50 minimum per year; ₹1.5 lakh maximum per financial year.","₹1,1000 minimum per year for Tier I; no upper limit on contribution"}","{Feature:"Lock-in / tenure","Linked to employment; full withdrawal is generally at retirement or after qualifying exit.","15 years initial lock-in; extendable in5-year blocks.","Locked until age 6 for normal exit."}","Withdrawals","Partial withdrawals allowed for specified needs; full withdrawal is generally tax-favoured after 5 years of continuous service","Partial withdrawal allowed from the 7th year; premature closure only after 5 completed years and for specified reasons.","Partial withdrawal allowed after 3 years, generally up to 25% of own contributions for specified purposes."}Treatment","Contributions may qualify for deduction under Section 80C; interest and eligible withdrawals are generally tax-free, but interest on employee contribution above ₹2.5 lakh/year can be taxable.","Contributions qualify under 80C; interest and maturity are generally fully tax-free.","Employee can get 80C benefit plus an extra ₹50000000 under 80CCD(1B; at exit, a lump sum portion is tax-annuity income is taxable."}Comparison","EPF","is the most employment-linked- and the least flexible but it offers strong tax tax efficiency for salaried workers.[1880]","PPF is the simplest low-risk option for residents seeking guaranteed, tax-free long-term savings, but it has a fixed annual cap and long-in.[9]","NPS is the most flexible for the for retirement accumulation because of its higher contribution ceiling and market-linked growth but its exit is partly taxable because annuity is regular income is taxed as regular income.[2]"
